{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLe5nWk7jpP3azVWEDDG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the SSRF issue with a one-line change at `lib/adapters/http.js:232`. When the Node HTTP adapter turns a relative URL into a full one, it now uses `platform.origin` as the base only in a browser-like environment. In plain Node it uses no base at all:\n\n```js\nconst parsed = new URL(fullPath, platform.hasBrowserEnv ? platform.origin : undefined);\n```\n\nBefore, it always used `'http://localhost'`, so a protocol-relative URL like `//google.com` silently picked up `http:` and sent a request to that other host. Now that request fails with `ERR_INVALID_URL`, which is how axios behaved before 1.3.2.\n\nI checked it by sending a request for `//google.com` through an instance with a `baseURL`: it was rejected with `ERR_INVALID_URL`. The existing HTTP adapter tests all pass (111).\n\nOne side effect: in Node, a plain relative path like `/foo` sent with no `baseURL` will now also throw `Invalid URL`. Before, it was quietly sent to `http://localhost`. The 1.3.2 fallback was added for #5458, so anyone relying on it will need to set a `baseURL`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":296,"cache_read_input_tokens":20723,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":296},"output_tokens":400,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"85bcee88-d19e-4889-b2f5-1a760d904736","uuid":"f74098af-331e-4dc4-98da-2f0dd3a0f87f","timestamp":"2026-11-23T22:22:00.476Z","request_id":"req_011ChLe5nR3Gyihyh9yvHjCU"}
